/*
RMSTEST_29 - what zone does set_zone_by_team give an UN-TEAMED player
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 14

QUESTION
guide:1056 gives a team's zone as TeamNumber - 9 and says nothing about team 0,
which is the engine's own value for un-teamed (guide:1002). Taken literally the
formula puts every un-teamed player in zone -9, which is ALSO player 1's own
default zone (playerNumber - 10). That would merge every un-teamed player's land
with player 1's and with each other's, so an FFA map that writes
set_zone_by_team collapses into one landmass.

Sec.6.1 currently pins the graceful alternative instead - un-teamed players keep
their default playerNumber - 10 - and says so explicitly as A CHOICE, NOT A
MEASUREMENT. This run turns it into one or the other. The fix is one line
whichever way it goes, but 4.2 should not be built on a guess.

METHOD
create_player_lands with set_zone_by_team and a large
other_zone_avoidance_distance. Zone membership is not directly visible in an
export, so the avoidance distance is the instrument: lands in DIFFERENT zones
push each other apart, lands in the SAME zone do not. Turn the invisible
property into a measurable gap.

The two configurations are the whole test and must both be run:
  29-ALL   every player un-teamed (FFA)
  29-MIX   players 1 and 2 on team 1, everyone else un-teamed

TEAMS ARE SET OUTSIDE THE SCRIPT. They come from the lobby, not from RMS. In
the scenario editor set them under the player/diplomacy settings before
generating. IF THE EDITOR WILL NOT LET YOU SET TEAMS FOR A RANDOM MAP, this one
has to be run from a real game lobby with the script as the selected map and the
result exported afterwards. Check this before committing a sitting to it - it is
the only script in the batch with a setup step the editor may not support, and
finding that out after generating everything else would waste the sitting.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200) WITH 8 PLAYERS, both configurations.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. 29-ALL, if un-teamed keeps the default zone (Sec.6.1's pinned choice):
   eight separate GRASS patches, well spaced, indistinguishable from a map with
   no set_zone_by_team at all.
2. 29-ALL, if the literal formula holds: all eight lands in one zone, so
   other_zone_avoidance_distance constrains nothing between them and they are
   free to touch. Expect FEWER THAN EIGHT patches, and in the extreme one large
   merged mass. Patch count is the reading.
3. 29-MIX is the control that makes 29-ALL interpretable. Players 1 and 2 share
   a real team and should touch each other regardless of which reading is true,
   so it proves the avoidance instrument is actually working. If 29-MIX shows 8
   separate patches then other_zone_avoidance_distance is not doing anything
   here and NEITHER configuration can be read - fix the instrument first.
4. Under the literal reading, 29-MIX is the sharper case: players 1-2 are in
   zone -8 while the six un-teamed sit in zone -9 WITH player 1. Player 1 would
   have to be in two zones at once, so watch specifically whether player 1's
   land merges with its teammate, with the un-teamed pool, or neither. Any of
   the three is informative and the third would mean the formula is not what
   either reading assumes.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS
  Patch COUNT answers the question. Centroid spacing is the corroboration:
  under separate zones the minimum centre-to-centre distance should exceed
  other_zone_avoidance_distance, under a shared zone it need not.

RUN EACH CONFIGURATION THREE TIMES. Two lands failing to touch in one seed
proves nothing; three seeds with no contact is a reasonable read.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain SNOW

  create_player_lands
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    number_of_tiles 300
    base_size 5
    clumping_factor 20
    border_fuzziness 0
    set_zone_by_team
    other_zone_avoidance_distance 20
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
